{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Red Team Infrastructure?

**Red team infrastructure is the temporary, authorized platform used to coordinate a
security exercise, enforce its boundaries, and preserve evidence.** It gives operators
a controlled way to run approved simulations while security teams observe how people,
processes, and technology respond.

Its most important invariant is simple: **every action must be attributable to an
approved engagement, constrained to approved targets, observable, and stoppable.** A
platform that is reliable but cannot enforce scope or produce trustworthy evidence is
not production-ready.

This lesson focuses on architecture and operations for sanctioned exercises. It does
not cover gaining access, hiding activity, or bypassing third-party controls. Review
[red teaming fundamentals](/fundamentals/red-teaming-fundamentals) first if scope,
rules of engagement, and exercise safety are unfamiliar.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Think of this platform as a short-lived internal service with unusually strict safety
requirements: strong identity, explicit policy, isolated networks, complete audit
records, an exercised stop path, and provable teardown.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Separate control, exercise, and evidence responsibilities

A red team platform coordinates people, policy, temporary services, and records. The
architecture is easier to reason about when control decisions, exercise traffic, and
evidence storage have separate owners and failure behavior.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Control plane" eyebrow="Authorize" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Authenticates operators, issues short-lived access, evaluates the current target
allowlist, records approvals, and exposes the emergency stop control.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Exercise plane" eyebrow="Execute" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Hosts only the temporary coordination services required by the approved exercise. It
has no implicit route to unrelated production networks.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Evidence plane" eyebrow="Observe" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Collects identity, policy decisions, service events, and timestamps into a protected
store that operators cannot silently rewrite.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% system-flow title="An authorized action crosses explicit gates" caption="The same engagement ID and operator identity should be present at every boundary so responders can reconstruct intent and outcome." %}
{% system-node title="Operator device" eyebrow="1. Identify" icon="users" tone="blue" %}
Uses managed access and a named human identity.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Access gateway" eyebrow="2. Authenticate" icon="lock" tone="green" %}
Issues short-lived, least-privilege credentials.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Scope policy" eyebrow="3. Authorize" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Checks target, time window, technique class, and engagement status.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Exercise service" eyebrow="4. Coordinate" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Runs the approved simulation workflow without owning policy.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Approved target" eyebrow="5. Exercise" icon="server" tone="cyan" %}
Receives only traffic allowed by the current engagement.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Evidence pipeline" eyebrow="6. Record" icon="storage" tone="green" %}
Persists the actor, decision, action, result, and correlated timestamps.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

The policy check belongs on the execution path, not only in a ticket or operator
runbook. Human approval starts the engagement; machine-enforced policy keeps each
action inside it.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Size from observable work, not server count

Capacity planning starts with the event stream the exercise must preserve. For a first
estimate, multiply active targets by events per target per minute. Apply a measured
peak factor, then compare that rate with healthy collector capacity after one expected
failure.

For example, 80 active targets at 8 events per minute produce about 10.7 events per
second on average. A 3x burst is 32 events per second. Two collectors that each sustain
45 events per second provide 90 events per second before a failure, but only 45 after
one collector is lost.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="80" label="Active targets" detail="Explicitly approved systems" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="10.7/s" label="Average events" detail="80 x 8 / 60" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric value="32/s" label="Modeled peak" detail="Average x 3 burst factor" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="45/s" label="Failure capacity" detail="One surviving collector" tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The estimate is a test plan, not a guarantee. Benchmark serialization, transport,
validation, storage acknowledgements, and query load together. Preserve enough
headroom for replay and incident investigation instead of sizing only for steady state.

{% interactive-block id="technology/red-team-infrastructure-calculator" dataFile="/api/content/technology/red-team-infrastructure/data/engagement-envelope.json" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Make safety controls independent of operator memory

Production depth comes from explicit contracts at each boundary. Convenience should
not allow one credential, host, or service failure to remove both execution and safety
controls.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Named, short-lived identity" icon="users" tone="blue" %}
Use individual accounts, managed devices, just-in-time roles, and session expiry.
Shared credentials prevent attribution and make revocation too broad.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Machine-enforced scope" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Compile the approved targets, time window, and action classes into policy. Default to
deny when the request cannot be matched to a current engagement.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Network containment" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Give each engagement dedicated segments and egress rules. Management, exercise, and
evidence paths should not share an unrestricted flat network.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Managed secrets" icon="lock" tone="amber" %}
Keep credentials outside images and source files. Issue them for one role and
engagement, rotate them during incidents, and revoke them at teardown.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Tamper-evident evidence" icon="storage" tone="cyan" %}
Separate evidence writers from readers and retention administrators. Record clock
health and immutable object versions so a timeline can be defended later.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Independent stop path" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
An authorized controller outside the exercise plane must be able to disable new work,
revoke access, and isolate services even when the main console is unavailable.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

At minimum, every event should carry an engagement ID, operator ID, target ID, policy
decision, action category, event time, receipt time, and correlation ID. Sensitive
payload content should be minimized or redacted; an audit trail does not justify
collecting unrelated secrets or personal data.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Design failure behavior before the exercise starts

A failure is not only an availability problem. It can create unscoped activity,
unattributed actions, missing evidence, or an inability to stop. Decide which paths
fail closed, which may buffer safely, and who can authorize recovery.

{% interactive-block id="technology/red-team-infrastructure-calculator" dataFile="/api/content/technology/red-team-infrastructure/data/control-plane-failures.json" /%}

Use these default responses:

- **Scope policy unavailable:** block new actions. Availability is less important than
  preventing unvalidated work.
- **Operator credential exposed:** revoke the affected identity, stop its sessions,
  rotate dependent secrets, and preserve the evidence timeline.
- **Collector unavailable:** continue only while a bounded, encrypted buffer can retain
  all required evidence; otherwise pause before creating an audit gap.
- **Evidence store unavailable:** buffer locally with a size and time limit. Reconcile
  sequence numbers after recovery and treat any gap as an incident.
- **Emergency stop invoked:** deny new work first, then isolate services, preserve
  records, and verify target-side activity has ended.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Redundancy does not replace a stop policy. Extra instances can keep an unsafe workflow
running just as effectively as a safe one.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Operate the platform as a short-lived production system

The platform lifecycle begins before deployment and ends only when access, resources,
and evidence have been reconciled. Automate the repeatable controls, while retaining a
named human owner for authorization and incident decisions.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Authorize the envelope" label="Approve" %}
Record owner, targets, exclusions, time window, allowed activity, data handling, stop
conditions, and responder contacts in a versioned manifest.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Provision isolation" label="Build" %}
Create engagement-specific accounts, network segments, secrets, policy, collectors,
and evidence storage from reviewed templates.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Prove controls" label="Validate" %}
Test an allowed target, a denied target, credential expiry, clock synchronization,
evidence delivery, alert routing, and the emergency stop path.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Run with telemetry" label="Operate" %}
Watch policy denials, active identities, collector lag, buffer occupancy, target health,
and changes to the approved manifest.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="5" title="Contain deviations" label="Respond" %}
Pause on scope ambiguity, revoke exposed credentials, preserve evidence, notify the
agreed contacts, and resume only through documented authorization.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="6" title="Reconcile and destroy" label="Close" %}
Stop activity, export evidence, revoke access, remove routes and resources, verify the
inventory is empty, and retain only approved records.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

Useful service-level indicators include policy-decision latency, denied-action count,
collector queue depth, oldest unshipped event age, evidence write errors, clock offset,
credential age, emergency-stop completion time, and teardown exceptions. Every alert
needs an owner and a specific response.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Encode authorization and evidence limits

Small dependency-free tools make the operating contract reviewable in CI and during
preflight. The first example validates that an engagement manifest has bounded time,
named owners, valid network ranges, explicit exclusions, and a stop contact.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/red-team-infrastructure/code/validate-engagement-manifest.py" language="python" title="Validate an engagement manifest" /%}

The second example calculates how many events a degraded collector path must buffer.
Run it with expected rates and outage duration, then test the same envelope against the
real pipeline before relying on the result.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/red-team-infrastructure/code/simulate-evidence-buffer.py" language="python" title="Model a bounded evidence buffer" /%}

Both examples model controls; they do not initiate network activity. In production,
sign manifests, store approvals separately from the exercise plane, and export metrics
to the organization responsible for oversight.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Review trade-offs with the safety invariant intact

Architecture choices can change cost and convenience, but not the requirement that
work remains attributable, in scope, observable, and stoppable.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Shared platform vs. per engagement" icon="boxes" tone="blue" %}
A shared control plane reduces operational duplication. Engagement-specific identities,
policy, networks, and storage prefixes still limit cross-exercise blast radius.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Availability vs. fail closed" icon="scale" tone="amber" %}
Control-plane unavailability may pause work. That is usually the correct trade when
continuing would remove scope or identity guarantees.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Evidence depth vs. privacy" icon="storage" tone="violet" %}
More data can improve reconstruction but increases exposure and review cost. Collect
the minimum fields needed for safety, attribution, and agreed learning goals.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Automation vs. independent approval" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Automate provisioning, validation, and teardown. Keep authorization changes and resume
decisions under separate, named approval.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Before declaring the platform ready, require evidence that:

- an out-of-scope target is denied at the execution boundary;
- a disabled operator loses active and future access within the stated objective;
- one collector or evidence-store outage does not silently lose required events;
- the stop path works without the primary operator console;
- clocks and correlation IDs produce one defensible timeline;
- target owners can distinguish approved exercise activity from a real incident;
- teardown finds no active identities, routes, compute, domains, secrets, or queues;
- retained evidence has an owner, expiry policy, access log, and deletion proof.
{% /section-card %}

